{"causes": ["The powerful noble lineage of the House of Albret provided Louis with the necessary aristocratic connections and influence for a high-ranking ecclesiastical career.", "The ongoing consolidation of French royal power under figures like Charles VII and Louis XI sought to integrate influential noble families into national and ecclesiastical administration.", "The Papacy's efforts to secure political alliances and financial support from European monarchs encouraged the appointment of cardinals from prominent national noble houses."], "effects": ["Louis d'Albret's cardinalate further enhanced the political and spiritual prestige of the House of Albret within France and on the European stage.", "His appointments to key bishoprics and later the College of Cardinals provided a channel for French royal influence within the Papal Curia during a critical period.", "His career contributed to the tradition of noble families holding significant power within the Catholic Church, shaping its governance and patronage networks."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "house-of-albret", "sourceName": "House of Albret", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "louis-dalbret", "targetName": "Louis d'Albret", "context": "Louis d'Albret was a prominent member of the powerful French noble House of Albret, which significantly influenced his career path."}, {"sourceSlug": "pope-pius-ii", "sourceName": "Pope Pius II", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "louis-dalbret", "targetName": "Louis d'Albret", "context": "Pope Pius II elevated Louis d'Albret to the cardinalate on December 18, 1461, significantly advancing his ecclesiastical standing."}, {"sourceSlug": "louis-dalbret", "sourceName": "Louis d'Albret", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "rome-italy", "targetName": "Rome, Italy", "context": "Louis d'Albret served as Cardinal-Deacon and participated in the Papal Conclave of 1464 in Rome, where he also died in 1465."}, {"sourceSlug": "louis-dalbret", "sourceName": "Louis d'Albret", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "papal-conclave-1464", "targetName": "Papal Conclave of 1464", "context": "Cardinal Louis d'Albret was one of the cardinals who participated in the Papal Conclave of 1464, which elected Pietro Barbo as Pope Paul II."}, {"sourceSlug": "louis-xi-of-france", "sourceName": "Louis XI of France", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "louis-dalbret", "targetName": "Louis d'Albret", "context": "Louis d'Albret, as a French cardinal, often served as a liaison and advocate for the interests of King Louis XI within the Papal Curia."}], "places": [{"name": "Aire-sur-l'Adour, France", "role": "Bishopric"}, {"name": "Cahors, France", "role": "Bishopric"}, {"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Cardinalate location"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Louis d'Albret", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "louis-dalbret"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1336245", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_d%27Albret"], "tags": ["France", "Religious Figures", "Europe", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Louis d'Albret's career as a cardinal demonstrates the enduring power of noble families within the late medieval Church and their critical role in shaping both French royal policy and papal diplomacy. His position facilitated connections between the French crown and the Papacy, influencing ecclesiastical appointments and political maneuvering.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-06T07:06:06Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31079290366", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Louis d'Albret (1422–1465) was a French Cardinal. He was a son of Charles II of Albret, Count of Dreux.", "newValue": "Louis d'Albret, born in 1422 into the formidable House of Albret, a dominant Gascon noble family, ascended to prominence as a French Cardinal, his career emblematic of the intricate interplay between aristocratic power, royal influence, and the Papacy in 15th-century Western Europe. As the son of Charles II of Albret, Count of Dreux, and Anne d'Armagnac, he leveraged his distinguished lineage to secure significant ecclesiastical appointments, including the bishoprics of Aire and Cahors, before his elevation to the cardinalate under Pope Pius II.

His ecclesiastical journey began with his appointment as Bishop of Aire in 1445, a position he held for fifteen years, followed by his transfer to the more influential See of Cahors in 1460. The pinnacle of his career arrived on December 18, 1461, when Pope Pius II, seeking to consolidate support among the French nobility and potentially influence French foreign policy, elevated him to Cardinal-Deacon of Santa Lucia in Orphea. This appointment placed d'Albret at the heart of papal politics, granting him a voice in the Curia and a role in shaping the Church's direction during a period of post-conciliar reform and burgeoning Renaissance humanism.

Louis d'Albret's cardinalate reinforced the Albret family's already substantial influence, extending their reach into the highest echelons of the Catholic Church. His participation in the Papal Conclave of 1464, which elected Pope Paul II, underscored his position as a significant figure in the College of Cardinals. His patronage of religious institutions and his role in French ecclesiastical administration contributed to the Church's organizational stability and provided a conduit for royal interests within the Papacy, even as his premature death in Rome on September 13, 1465, cut short a potentially longer and more impactful career."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-06T07:06:06Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31079290366", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1423, France"}, {"title": "Spiritual seeking and religious community formation", "type": "Idea", "year": "1423, France"}], "newValue": ["The powerful noble lineage of the House of Albret provided Louis with the necessary aristocratic connections and influence for a high-ranking ecclesiastical career.", "The ongoing consolidation of French royal power under figures like Charles VII and Louis XI sought to integrate influential noble families into national and ecclesiastical administration.", "The Papacy's efforts to secure political alliances and financial support from European monarchs encouraged the appointment of cardinals from prominent national noble houses."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-06T07:06:06Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31079290366", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1465-09-13"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1465-09-13"}], "newValue": 